"executive_summary": "This study presents high-speed, multi-spectral lunar occultation observations of the carbon-rich AGB star AFGL 5440, conducted over the 1–4µm wavelength range using the pMIRAS instrument. The primary goal was to resolve the spatial structure of the circumstellar dust shell, which had previously been inferred only from spectral energy distributions (SEDs). By fitting the observed diffraction fringes with radiative transfer models (DUSTY), the authors constrained the inner radius of the dust shell to a temperature of 950 K ± 50 K and an optical depth of 0.5 ± 0.1 at 5µm. The data strongly support a density gradient of ρ ∝ r⁻², consistent with radiatively driven hydrodynamic outflows. A significant finding is that models fitting the near-infrared occultation data and SED fail to reproduce the far-infrared (60µm) flux unless a substantial abundance of graphite is included or a significantly higher historical mass-loss rate is assumed. The study successfully separates the stellar photosphere (angular diameter ~3 mas) from the inner edge of the dust shell (angular radius ~11 mas), providing direct constraints on the recent dust mass-loss rate of approximately 6.5 × 10⁻⁸ M⊙/yr.",
